{"number": 130, "text": "            Exercises\n\n            Listen. What do Sue and Jim think about the people they are speaking about? Write the names in the\n            correct column.\n\n             people they like                                 people they don't like\n                                                              deremy\n\n            SUE: Jeremy  is going to stay at the same hotel as us.\n            Jim: Oh  great!\n            Jim: Anne’s  invited us to a party.\n            SUE: Fantastic!\n            Jim: Kathleen’s coming  to stay with us for a few days.\n            SUE:         be fun!\n            Sue: Gail and  Tim want  to come  on holiday with  us.\n            Jim: That’ll be nice!\n\n            You will hear Claire (from part B on the opposite\n            page) giving her opinion about these things on\n            their holiday. Imagine you are Paul. Listen and\n            say stronger opinions. There are gaps on the\n            recording for you to speak. Don't forget to make\n            your voice high.\n\n\n            EXAMPLE:\n            You  hear Claire say The beach  was quite nice. You say Nice? It was absolutely fantastic!\n\n            The responses to the pairs of sentences a and b below are the same, but the speaker pronounces\n            them  differently. For example, in the response to Example a, the voice is flat, but in the response to\n            Example  b, the voice goes high. Draw lines to show if you think the voice will be flat or go high.\n            Then  listen and check.\n            EXAMPLE\n               a — We had  to stay in a five-star hotel.      b — We  had to spend  two days in the airport.\n                 — How  awful  for you! —»>                     — How   awful for you!\n            1  a — Forget the beach; it’s raining again!    1 b—  They  say we don’t have to pay; it’s free.\n                 — Brilliant!                                   — Brilliant!\n            2  a—I  got an A  in the exam!                  2         crashed  the car again!\n                 — Well done!                                   — Well done!\n            3  a —I can count  to three in German.          3 b—I   learnt how to fly a plane while we were\n                 — Amazing!                                       on holiday.\n                                                                — Amazing!\n            4  a       could pick fresh fruit off the trees 4     We  had a tiny bit of cheese on a dry, old\n                   in the garden.                                 piece of bread.\n                 — Delicious!                                   — Delicious!\n             5 a—Frank   says he’ll take us to the airport. 5     The  car’s broken down  and there are no\n                 ~ Excellent!                                     taxis.\n                                                                — Excellent!\n\n                                                                          English Pronunciation in Use     129", "char_count": 2988, "mean_conf": 92.11, "word_count": 376, "words": [{"text": "Exercises", "x0": 233, "y0": 46, "x1": 558, "y1": 111, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "Listen.", "x0": 229, "y0": 189, "x1": 340, "y1": 222, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "What", "x0": 356, "y0": 188, "x1": 454, "y1": 221, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "do", "x0": 470, "y0": 188, "x1": 512, "y1": 220, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "Sue", "x0": 529, "y0": 187, "x1": 593, "y1": 220, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "and", "x0": 609, "y0": 186, "x1": 673, "y1": 219, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "Jim", "x0": 689, "y0": 185, "x1": 749, "y1": 219, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "think", "x0": 767, "y0": 184, "x1": 860, "y1": 219, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "about", "x0": 876, "y0": 184, "x1": 981, "y1": 218, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "the", "x0": 997, "y0": 183, "x1": 1054, "y1": 217, 